(PRC-34 — the no-argument form synthesizes argv = [path, NULL], argc 1.
 /bin/echo with no arguments prints one empty line; with any argument it
 would print that argument, so the single empty line is the observation.
 Hand-verified more directly with a Vox helper that prints its own argv,
 which is not retained here because Execute needs a compiled target and
 this probe directory holds source. The helper was:

   a text called marker is environment's "PRC_MARKER".
   print "argv0={arguments's name}".
   print "argc={arguments's count}".
   For each 'the argument' in arguments's raw,
       print "arg={'the argument'}".
   print "MARKER={marker}".

 and `Execute "./argvdump.bin".` printed exactly:
   argv0=./argvdump.bin
   argc=1
   MARKER=inherited2
 confirming both argc 1 and that argv[0] is the path (PRC-40).
 NOTE: /bin/echo's empty line IS printed, but it is the last thing on
 stdout and command substitution strips trailing newlines, so the
 recorded block below ends at the probe's own line. docs/check-probes.sh
 captures the actual output the same way, so the two still match.
 Ran, from the repo root:
   VOX_CORE_PATH=/home/josj/scr/english/vox/coreasm \
   /home/josj/scr/english/vox/target/release/vox \
   docs/ledger/probes/process-control/PRC-34.vox -o p && ./p
 expected output:
   the next line is all /bin/echo printed:
)
print "the next line is all /bin/echo printed:".
Execute "/bin/echo".
On error print "the exec failed".
